inl note_of_notebooks_to_spi_siblings_8 () = Note ("NOTEBOOKS-TO-SPI-SIBLINGS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: all 7 .livemd deletions are in HEAD (dice, alphabet, polyglot) and their CI is green on top.")
inl note_of_swift_warm_compile_1 () = Note ("SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:50Z", "main-thread", "08:50 measured (swiftc 6.4, machine loaded by a suite): a trivial 'import CRT; exit(0)' costs 1.1-1.6 s with -c alone (stdlib/SDK module loading); real rows cost 2.1-3.8 s with -O, of which linking is 0.2-0.4 s. -Onone is not faster (2.7-8.5 s). So about half of each row is fixed per-invocation overhead, and batching is the lever. swiftc cannot emit many executables in one call, and top-level code is only legal in main.swift, so batching needs a codegen mode that wraps the program as 'func spiralMain_<id>() -> Int32'. The harness would then compile every Swift row into one module and one exe that dispatches on argv, the way the Kotlin server does. Next: that codegen flag, then time one batched build against the per-row sum.")
inl note_of_swift_warm_compile_2 () = Note ("SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE", 2u32, "2026-10-14T12:55Z", "main-thread", "08:55 prototype (job tmp/rotation/swiftwarm/batch_probe.ps1), 12 samples. A harness-side text wrap needs no codegen change: drop 'import CRT' and 'exit(spiral_main())', wrap each file in 'enum Spiral<i> { ... }' with func/var/let made static (types and protocols nest), plus one main.swift that switches on argv[1]. All 12 exit codes agree with the separate builds. Build time: separate -O 42.2 s; batched -O (WMO) 42.6 s; WMO -num-threads 4 35.9 s; -O -no-whole-module-optimization -j 4 13.5 s (3.1x). Next: a test.ps1 pre-pass like the Kotlin server (one no-WMO batch build before the lanes, lanes run all.exe <i>, per-row fallback if the batch fails), then measure Swift's suite share (10.5% today).")
inl note_of_swift_warm_compile_3 () = Note ("SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE", 3u32, "2026-10-14T15:04Z", "main-thread", "11:04 landed in test.ps1 and verified for correctness: Swift pre-pass with per-row fallback; single-flight-20261014-104919 C,Swift examples has 248/248 Swift rows agreeing, DISAGREE 0. First cut was one 248-program module, 557 s, slower than per-row builds (~470 s over 2 lanes) because one module's type-check grows faster than linear. Fix: Swift files import per file (CRT on each wrapped file); now chunks of 16 programs, one exe each, built in parallel with -no-whole-module-optimization -j 2. Verify run queued. results.tsv run_ms shows a Swift row's run is 133 ms of its 3,781 ms.")
inl note_of_swift_warm_compile_4 () = Note ("SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE", 4u32, "2026-10-14T15:15Z", "main-thread", "11:15 done and measured: test.ps1 builds all Swift rows of a native run as 16-program chunks (one exe each; parallel across native lanes; -no-whole-module-optimization -j 2; per-row fallback for a failed chunk). single-flight-20261014-110801, C,Swift examples, -Parallel 2, on a busy machine: 248/248 programs batched in 172.5 s, 248 Swift rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0. Native tier 313.9 s vs 547.3 s with per-row builds (single-flight-20261014-102211), 1.74x on the whole C+Swift tier. Run time is now only the exe run (median ~133 ms).")
inl note_of_swift_warm_compile_5 () = Note ("SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:27Z", "main-thread", "06:27 main: Done. 16-program chunked Swift builds have been in test.ps1 since 10-14 and every full suite since uses them (blesses 233702, 015332, 041405, 052820: Swift rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0). Remaining is only your commit of test.ps1.")
inl note_of_swift_warm_compile_6 () = Note ("SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE", 6u32, "2026-10-15T10:27Z", "main-thread", "06:27 correction to the previous note: test.ps1 with the chunked Swift builds is already committed (spiral 570be6a6, 10-14 21:31), so nothing is left for the user.")
inl note_of_probe_matrix_run_1 () = Note ("PROBE-MATRIX-RUN", 1u32, "2026-10-14T10:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 06:59 review: partly superseded: the 2026-10-14 night ran test.ps1 -Probe Bend over every C sample (343 then 363 Bend rows agree); the matrix is still due for Wasm/Zig/Lean/Gleam/Lua/TypeScript; reference is now tonight's blessed oracle (single-flight-20261014-043715), not dev41")
inl note_of_probe_matrix_run_2 () = Note ("PROBE-MATRIX-RUN", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: superseded in practice: test.ps1 -Probe plus the widened harness lists cover the matrix (19 backends in every full run). Lowered P2 -> P3; close once a -Probe run over the 259 portable samples is recorded for Zig.")
inl note_of_probe_matrix_run_3 () = Note ("PROBE-MATRIX-RUN", 3u32, "2026-10-15T22:06Z", "main-thread", "18:06 main: done by per-backend probes on the installed compiler (test.ps1 -Probe <backend> on a copied tree, examples suite, rows kept when they agree with C and run <= 3 s): Wasm 150->393, Lua 140->429, Zig 147->441 (pack-1k/1l), TypeScript 97->413, Gleam 129->412, Lean 137->413, Python new list 383 (pack-1n). Kotlin/Swift (248 each) next; generic script job tmp/pack-n/list-growth.ps1.")
inl note_of_probe_matrix_run_4 () = Note ("PROBE-MATRIX-RUN", 4u32, "2026-10-15T22:59Z", "main-thread", "18:59 main: pack-1n blessed (single-flight-20261015-183509 = review 180548, 6,274 rows, 1,258 added rows all agree). Kotlin and Swift probes running for pack-1o.")
inl note_of_probe_matrix_run_5 () = Note ("PROBE-MATRIX-RUN", 5u32, "2026-10-16T00:06Z", "main-thread", "20:06 main: pack-1o blessed (single-flight-20261015-194349, 6,507 rows): Kotlin 248 -> 389, Swift 248 -> 340. Every general-purpose backend now has 340-441 rows.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_1 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:36Z", "main-thread", "08:36 first comparison from single-flight-20261014-073517 (145 rows where both Zig and C ran natively): median Zig/C native_ms 1.27, totals Zig 654 s vs C 326 s. On the megas Zig is faster on 4 (inventory_closed_dfa 4.6 vs 6.0 s, lean_cic 1.5 vs 3.3, spiral_proves 2.2 vs 3.2, brzozowski runtime_native 3.7 vs 4.6) and slower on 4 (runtime_bench 14.4 vs 9.6, omniledger 4.0 vs 2.6, zeta 2.5 vs 1.5, indexed_dfa 5.4 vs 3.6); cube 2.2 vs 1.6. native_ms mixes build and run under suite load, so this is not yet a run-speed verdict. Next: record build_ms and run_ms separately in the harness, then rerun the megas quiet.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_2 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:25Z", "main-thread", "17:25 first run-only comparison (the new run_ms column, from single-flight-20261014-164608, a 2-lane suite on a busy machine; run_ms includes process start): median run_ms vs C on the same rows: Zig 0.96 (145 rows, sum 11.1 s vs C 10.8 s), Rust 1.16 (409), Swift 1.54 (248), Wasm 2.66 (149), Lua 4.07 (137), Kotlin 9.4 (248, JVM start), Bend 28 (344), Lean 89 (137, lean --run elaboration). Zig megas against C: runtime_bench 166 vs 523 ms, spiral_proves 68 vs 153, zeta 105 vs 202, lean_cic 53 vs 65, cube 24 vs 80 (Zig faster); omniledger 202 vs 45, indexed_dfa 166 vs 117, inventory_dfa 145 vs 89 (Zig slower); brzozowski runtime_native about equal. So Zig is at C speed overall but not everywhere. Next: a quiet -Parallel 1 rerun of the megas, 3 repeats, then profile omniledger on Zig.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_3 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:49Z", "main-thread", "17:49 clean run-speed result: min of 7 direct runs of the harness-built binaries (System.Diagnostics.Process, every exit correct; C gcc as built by the harness, Zig ReleaseFast, Rust release), ms C/Zig/Rust: brzozowski runtime_bench 333/87/346, indexed_dfa 78/30/79, inventory_dfa 14/13/27, runtime_native 13/11/14, lean_cic 11/11/11, omniledger 18/10/14, spiral_proves 16/13/19, zeta 15/13/22, cube 14/10/18. Zig matches or beats C on every program: 3.8x faster on runtime_bench and 2.6x on indexed_dfa; the rest are within process-start noise (~10-18 ms). The two big wins come from one sample family. So 'Zig hub only at C speed' is met on run time; the remaining hub criteria are coverage (Zig list 146 rows vs C 509) and build time (~2 s per program). Caveat: the earlier suite-embedded run_ms numbers were dominated by background load (1-3 s swings) and are not usable for speed claims.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_4 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: speed criterion met (Zig >= C on all 9, 2-4x on the heavy two; 5-7.5x over compiled F#). What remains is coverage: Zig 146 rows vs C 513, lib arms in 9 of 195 files. Work moves to PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES / BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_5 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:56Z", "main-thread", "06:56 groom: progress 50 -> 60: the speed half is met (measured 10-14 and 10-15); the coverage half is carried by PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES (P0) and BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS (P1).")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_6 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-10-15T11:15Z", "main-thread", "07:15 build-time half measured: with a warm global cache, Zig builds small programs in ~0.3 s (zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146), about 2x faster than C's gcc -O2 at 0.56 s; the cold first build is 8.9-15 s. The harness gives each run a fresh cache, so its native_ms overstates Zig builds; a shared cache for the harness would cut suite time too.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_7 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-10-16T17:19Z", "main-thread", "13:19 main: pack-1w installed (12:46/12:52) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-125241, 6,888 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against pack-1v's 112838 overridden by the Zig/Gleam/Lean/Wasm candidate probes and the Lua/TypeScript census runs. Fixes: Zig unit macros as statements (while_control, while_loop, native_cube_*), Gleam multi-subject match wildcards, Lean Inhabited for IO.Ref (heap layouts with arrays), fixture byte_to_i32 (Gleam braces, Wasm arm). Lists +130: Zig 455, Gleam 438, Lean 432, Wasm 441, Lua 439, AlsoTypeScript 427.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_8 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 8u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: two fixes staged and reviewed: zig-reserved-stack (spiralMain on a CreateThread 1 GB reservation instead of std.Thread committing it: native_cube 14.7 -> 4.7 ms, a 3M-frame recursion still runs) and zig-float-render (float printing through std.fmt.float.render: an f64-printing program builds in 1.45 s instead of 2.6 s, same as i32). With both, Zig runs at C speed on the 9-program bench (Zig/C 0.43-1.14, median ~0.95; was 2.5-2.9 on every small program), all exits matching C (tmp/night/bench-native-candidate.tsv). The hub condition is met on this set; scored as oracle, Zig agrees on 5,354 rows with 67 samples lacking a Zig row and one bounds sample where Zig is stricter than C (CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE); the switch is the user call. Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_9 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 9u32, "2026-10-17T14:38Z", "main-thread", "10:38 main-thread day: Zig as the oracle was measured on the installed pack: -Oracle Zig gives DISAGREE 0 over the whole suite (agree 5,354; under C 5,383), and 67 rows are no-zig. The only divergent sample is dynamic_array_bounds_negative: Zig, Lean, Bend and Gleam trap the bad index with exit 3, while C, Delphi, Wasm, Kotlin and Lua read 0 unchecked. Those Known entries are now oracle-scoped. A fidelity bug found by the study lane is fixed in the tree: Zig's main exited through std.process.exit(u8), so on Windows an exit code of 305 became 49. It now calls kernel32 ExitProcess with the full i32 (POSIX keeps std.process.exit, which truncates like C). Regression sample samples/zig_exit_wide, on the Zig list only (WASI exit statuses stop at 126). Patch: lab/zig-hub-speed/zig-wide-exit.ps1 (-DryRun). Review single-flight-20261017-103303 (-ReuseNative, about 5 min): exactly the 457 Zig residual cells plus the 5 new rows, DISAGREE 0. The bless is next.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_10 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 10u32, "2026-10-17T15:47Z", "main-thread", "11:47 main-thread: the wide-exit fix is blessed (single-flight-20261017-111908, 6,915 rows, DISAGREE 0; zig_exit_wide: Zig 305 = C) and installed in all 3 modes.")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_11 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 11u32, "2026-10-17T15:56Z", "main-thread", "11:56 main-thread: Zig is now an equivalent oracle. 38 samples have no Zig row by nature: C-ABI shims (abi_external_*, dynamic_array_*, native_managed_array_scc*), C-only macros, and samples written for eBPF, Rust or Python. Adding all 38 to the Zig list gave 15 compile errors and 23 C-only build failures, so I reverted it. test.ps1 now falls back to the C row when the oracle backend has no row for a sample (detail 'oracle C (no Zig row)'). With that, -Oracle Zig on the blessed pack (single-flight-20261017-115212, -ReuseNative) gives agree 5,388 and DISAGREE 0, the same as -Oracle C, with 31 rows verified through the fallback. Zig runs at C speed (night bench, Zig/C median ~0.95), and its exit codes now match C's. Decision recorded: the default stays C. Switching it changes bless semantics for every lane, so it goes to the user with this data (one parameter default plus a bless under -Oracle Zig).")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_12 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 12u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: lab/zig-hub-speed deleted (zig-wide-exit.ps1 was the applied one-shot edit for the Zig ExitProcess fix, regression sample samples/zig_exit_wide; build-and-review-modes.ps1 is covered by build.ps1 + test.ps1).")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_13 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 13u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:21 lap 1 grain: Zig has the fewest gaps after C on today's review (65 C samples without a Zig row, 1 row not ok); 38 of the 65 are C-by-nature (ABI shims, C macros, eBPF/Rust/Python-specific), so about 27 are closable (ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY); the remaining epic gate is ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT (85%) Next: HEAVY QUEUE: replay pack 5 with -Oracle Zig,C -ReuseNative to confirm DISAGREE 0 on the nightly + wgpu 30 tree (ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE)")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_14 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 14u32, "2026-10-19T13:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:02 lap 2 grain: the per-backend-key review job (review-keys, started 08:58) runs -Oracle Zig,C over the full suite with every native row fresh, which is this item's queued Zig-first-oracle check; Zig's remaining coverage gap is led by the DynamicArray* C-runtime family (33 macro uses in its 65 missing samples, PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES note) Next: read review-keys round A: DISAGREE under Zig as first oracle")
inl note_of_zig_hub_speed_15 () = Note ("ZIG-HUB-SPEED", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 closed: both remaining gates met, ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT Done (10-19) and Zig is the first oracle in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged), ZIG-FLIP-OK).")
inl note_of_eoie_regen_drift_1 () = Note ("EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", 1u32, "2026-10-14T15:00Z", "main-thread", "11:00 publish attempt after the bless (test-regeneration.ps1 -Test -Publish, deployed DLL) did not publish. Its build.ps1 test stage stopped when flat_bundle_roundtrip_is_self_verified hit the 900 s command timeout, with the machine loaded by a compiler build and a Swift suite; the other cold-owner, receipt and planning suites passed. The eoie tree is unchanged by it (only the lanes' 4 edited files differ). Rerun on a quiet machine after pack-1c. pack-1c changes no Rust codegen, so the regeneration stays valid across it.")
inl note_of_eoie_regen_drift_2 () = Note ("EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", 2u32, "2026-10-14T19:09Z", "main-thread", "15:09 second publish attempt (deployed pack-1d DLL) also stopped in the -Test stage: build.ps1's 900 s command timeout during the cold cargo build of all owners, with a compiler build loading the machine; nothing published. Plan: test-regeneration.ps1 -Publish without -Test, then eoie build.ps1 -Test -SkipRelease as the validation, which has no 900 s cap (267 test binaries, 199 tests per lane B this morning). If validation fails, restore the published owners from the staged copies. Runs after the pack-1e review suite so neither disturbs the other.")
inl note_of_eoie_regen_drift_3 () = Note ("EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", 3u32, "2026-10-14T20:44Z", "main-thread", "16:44 handed to lane B, which is working in eoie after the pack-1e install: publish without -Test, then build.ps1 -Test -SkipRelease as the validation, then a second regeneration that must report 0 changed owners.")
inl note_of_eoie_regen_drift_4 () = Note ("EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", 4u32, "2026-10-14T22:00Z", "lane-b", "lane B publish on the installed pack-1e (uncommitted, eoie): test-regeneration -IncludeAuxiliary -CargoCheck -Publish: 89 owners changed, 56 unchanged (96 files, +3,853/-3,615); build.ps1 -Test -SkipRelease: 267 test binaries, 199 tests, 0 failed; a second regeneration published 0 changed / 145 unchanged; largest crate 941 lines (under the 1000 gate)")
inl note_of_eoie_regen_drift_5 () = Note ("EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:27Z", "main-thread", "06:27 main: Done. A second regeneration publishes 0 changed owners, and tonight's renewal on pack-1g (04:57, ready=True) covers the regenerated sources. Remaining is only your commit.")
inl note_of_eoie_regen_drift_6 () = Note ("EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", 6u32, "2026-10-26T01:10Z", "eoie", "21:10 eoie: test-regeneration.ps1's stale-workspace prune (lines 37-38, folders older than 2 days) fails silently. spiral-bin\\eoie-regeneration holds 67 run folders, 6.6 GB, the oldest from 10-23 15:49, which SLATE's regen start on 10-25 should have removed. The catch only prints with Write-Host. Fix after KIT-RENAME (the renamed script writes kit-regeneration, so the old folder is deleted whole then): make the prune report the failure in the run's summary and retry with attributes cleared; a failed run should also say where its staged tree stayed. Asked by main (DISK-CLEANUP).")
