inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_2 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: XC005 overweight staged and verified: pack-c/xcompat-weight.ps1 (twitter-text v3 weights over the compiler TLD list and xCompatLinks; block = column-0 line to the next). Candidate --x-compat: x/apps/client 0 findings (max 280), blog/engine 10 overweight; for scale lib/spiral 487 and apps/tibia 209 over (not edition 3). Lands with PACK-NIGHT-1017. Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_3 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 3u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x/apps/client/build.ps1 now runs the compiler's --x-compat (XC005 weight) report-only after the gate: 0 findings in 18 files; the scratch weight script is retired. It caught 19 overweight blocks and 8 autolinks (e.author: author is a TLD) in new code, all fixed")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_4 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 4u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 compiler side done: XC005 shipped in PACK-NIGHT-1017 and x/apps/client/build.ps1 runs it (0 findings). Cells side missing: Spiral.Cells.XCompat.rewrites reports hashtag/mention/cashtag/autolink only, no block weight; it closes with X-COMPAT-CHECK's last step (Cells calls the compiler's check instead of its own port)")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_5 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:51 headroom finding from posting x's own sources: XC005 allows 280 per top-level block, but a posted block also carries its CELL k/n header line (about 7 to 9 weight, e.g. 'c6 1/2' + newline) and +1 per defused link dot, so a block at 273+ spills into a second post (checks.spi main: 273 + '0i32' alone in post 2). For one post per definition the postable limit is 280 minus the header, about 271")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_6 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 6u32, "2026-10-19T01:03Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 21:03 dogfood check in x: apps/client/build.ps1 now runs xc post-thread --dry-run over every client source after the e2e (report-only, ~15 s) and names each cell that needs more than one post by its first line; today 885 posts for 21 files, 16 files with cells over one post (45 cells), which XC005 cannot see because it does not count the CELL k/n header line or the defused link dots")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_7 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 7u32, "2026-10-19T01:25Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 21:25 first fixes from the postability report: checks.spi main (named username_or_empty), jsonl.spi json_lines (lines_of) and thread.spi paged (bodies alias) now fit one post with their header; 43 cells left over one post in 885 posts. args command_fields and net send_reply are the hard ones (long record type, long Rust expression)")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_8 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 8u32, "2026-10-19T01:36Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 21:36 fetch.spi gather (gathered_one) and users.spi pages (page_of) fit one post too: 41 cells over one post left, 11 files (was 16); full client build green")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_9 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 9u32, "2026-10-20T03:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:38 main, request from the x lane (P3, rides the next core-file pack): XC005 (spiral_compiler.fs xCompatOverweightFindings, xCompatPostWeightLimit) checks each top-level block against 280, but a posted block also carries its cN k/n header line and costs +1 per defused link dot, so 40 x client cells pass XC005 and still spill into a second post (37 of them by 20 weight or less, measured with xc post-thread --dry-run). Rule matching xc's splitter: limit for block i (1-based) = 280 - weight(c + i + ' 1/1' + newline) - defused dots, where a dot is defused when the glyph before it is a label char and the one after it is a letter (x/apps/client/splitter.spi posted_unit, glyphs.spi label_char and letter). Message: weight W > L (280 minus the cN k/n header and defused dots). Proof plan: a lab patch with --apply, then the 40 cells must show up as findings in the x client; x fixes the cells itself.")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_10 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 10u32, "2026-10-20T04:55Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 00:55 x side, uncommitted: (1) splitter.spi chunks_of and sim frames.ex split_units start from a one-digit post count (9, was 99), so a one-post cell budgets 'cN 1/1' instead of 'cN 99/99' (2 weight per cell); a cell needing 10+ posts still re-splits with the wider header (new tests one_post_cells_budget_a_one_digit_header, ten_posts_widen_the_header); (2) the other 31 cells reshaped by helpers and renames (cli valued/usernames_in/unknown_option/dates_text/thread_text, wire following_page record for FollowedBy, distinct_parts, refused, unreached, next_page_doc, posts entry_at/liked, oauth1 consumer_params/token_params, transport keys_of, token failed_reply, write first_posting, xc found_plan/viewed and window since/until, args since/until with the crate tuple order unchanged). A scratchpad estimator of the splitter's rule (calibrated: same 16 cells as xc on wire/posts/xc) reports 0 cells over one post; build verification pending")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_11 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 11u32, "2026-10-20T08:06Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 04:06 x side done and verified (x-chain5): postable 1101 posts for 30 files, 0 cells over one post (was 40 cells in 11 files); x-compat 0 in 30 files. Left: XC005's postable budget in the compiler (sent to main 10-20 00:5x) and the Cells-side weight check (X-COMPAT-CHECK last step)")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_12 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 12u32, "2026-10-20T22:54Z", "main", "18:54 main: the compiler side of x's 10-19 23:38 request is written as lab X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT/postable-budget.ps1 (dry run matches both anchors; shared section, both cores). Block i (1-based top-level block) is checked against 280 - weight('c<i> 1/1' + newline) - defused dots, where a dot is defused when the char before is an ASCII letter, digit, _ or - and the char after is an ASCII letter (x splitter.spi posted_unit, glyphs.spi label_char and letter). Message: 'weight W > L (280 minus the cN k/n header and defused dots)'. Proof queued as heavy job x-budget: installed vs candidate --x-compat on x/apps/client now (expect 0 XC005; x fixed its cells) and on HEAD copies from before x's 00:55 fixes (expect about 40 new XC005 under the candidate). Follow-up: the host's runXCompatReport walks directories with SearchOption.AllDirectories, the same unpruned walk that looped on deps symlinks in the formatter.")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_13 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 13u32, "2026-10-20T23:42Z", "main", "19:42 proof (heavy job x-budget, Debug candidate core 87B3A977D3497AA0 with the tree host incl. host-walk.ps1): --x-compat on x/apps/client HEAD (61b8fff, before x's 00:55 cell fixes, 27 files) gives installed 0 findings vs candidate 29 overweight. On the current client (36 files) both give 0, which agrees with x's xc post-thread dry run (0 cells over one post). 29 rather than the 40 measured on 10-19 fits the rule: x measured 40 with its old 'cN 99/99' header budget, and since 00:55 the splitter, and so this rule, budgets the one-digit 'cN 1/1'. The host walk fix holds: '--x-compat C:\\home\\git\\dice' (deps\\polyglot symlink in a link cycle) finishes in 1 s (396 findings, dice is not edition 3). Ships in the evening pack with candidate 8 and published-ready.")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_14 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 14u32, "2026-10-21T01:44Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:44 x-lane backlog refactor: the x side is done (0 cells over one post in 36 files); what is left is main's: installing the proven postable budget (lab postable-budget.ps1, candidate 87B3A977) and the Cells-side weight check, so the 30m estimate is the install plus one x-compat rerun on the client from the x lane")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_15 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 15u32, "2026-10-21T03:16Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 23:16 done for x (X-DONE deliberation 1): the client keeps 0 cells over one post and 0 --x-compat findings, re-proven by the pre-commit round (postable 1617 posts in 36 files, 0 over). The compiler half moved to the main-owned XC005-POSTABLE-BUDGET; the Cells-side weight check stays with X-COMPAT-CHECK's last step")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_16 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 16u32, "2026-10-21T07:20Z", "main", "2026-10-21 03:20 main: postable budget (XC005 limit = 280 - weight of the c{i} 1/1 marker - defused dots) and the link-cycle-safe host walks INSTALLED with the evening pack at 03:19 (tree core A9A71772B249FB8B). x told to rerun --x-compat; matrix review running.")
inl note_of_x_compat_block_weight_17 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT", 17u32, "2026-10-22T02:23Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:23 x: lab assimilated. spiral/lab/X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT held two compiler patch scripts (Old/New text pairs applied to spiral_compiler.fs and host Program.fs). Each pair was checked against today's compiler sources (1,252 .fs/.spi/.spir files under apps/compiler). postable-budget.ps1: the overweight explanation landed verbatim; the overweight-finding hunk landed in an evolved form (xCompatDefusedDots, xCompatPostableLimit and xCompatOverweightFindings exist at spiral_compiler.fs 182781-182791, now taking a PostabilityProfile, from X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE). host-walk.ps1: all three New texts are present (filesUnder with link-cycle-safe ResolveLinkTarget, and the skip list target, node_modules, deps, bin, obj, _build, .git, .history); the Old texts are gone, except the sourceGraphFingerprint header, which the New text keeps. Nothing unlanded, so the folder is deleted and the item is Done again.")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_1 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:25Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 03:25 read side done and tested offline: crates xc_thread (root + pages, 411 lines) and xc_assemble (frames -> cells, 936); the e2e stub serves two threads the simulator posted (mix xsim.world: prelude.spi 3 cells / 8 posts; posts.spi 13 cells / 111 posts with 3 edit rounds, editor and breaker personas, one lost frame), and xc thread rebuilds both byte for byte against the sim's record (incomplete cell named, exit 4, second page via next_token). Contract read live (~\\.12): search/recent returns the root for from:+conversation_id:, newest first, meta.next_token paging, edit_history_post_ids on every post, numeric from: works, a missing post is 200 + errors. Left: compiling the rebuilt program")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_2 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T08:35Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 04:35 correction: the stress thread is 113 posts, not 111; the earlier fixture lost the breaker persona to the mix.bat comma bug (see SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM); regenerated with editor=2+breaker=1, e2e still byte-exact")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_3 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 e2e sim fixtures are now diffable: Xsim.XWorld.json indents 4 levels (one post per line, keys sorted) and expected.program is an array of source lines; sim-stress.world.json 1 -> 124 lines, sim-stress.expected.json 1 -> 85; parsed content identical to the single-line form, drift guard and e2e pass. Left: run the rebuilt program (needs a design call: xc shells out to the Spiral compiler/Cells, or hands the source to Cells as a notebook)")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_4 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:02Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 09:02 coverage gap closed: --lost always dropped p2, the only multi-frame cell of posts.spi (48 frames, edited into 94 posts), so the stress check only covered 12 one-post cells. sim-stress now loses nothing and exits 0 (115 posts, program 1,975 -> ~12,000 chars, p2 rebuilt from its latest versions byte for byte), and a new sim-lost world (prelude.spi 3 cells, p2 frame 2 lost) keeps the exit-4 case; apps/sim/build.ps1 -Bless rewrites the fixtures after the posted sources change")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_5 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T13:02Z", "claude", "correction to note 4: the stress program is now 14,787 chars (measured), not ~12,000")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_6 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T13:51Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 09:51 bless paths are in the repo now: apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1 -Bless rewrites the README walkthrough outputs (proven: two perturbed outputs fail without it and are restored byte for byte with it), apps/sim/build.ps1 -Bless rewrites the sim fixtures; the scratch bless script is retired")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_7 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 7u32, "2026-10-18T16:32Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 12:32 threads of any age: search/recent only sees 7 days and returned 0 (exit 0, empty program) for an older thread. Live contract (~$0.17, 34 posts): search/all works on our plan, defaults to the last 30 days unless start_time is sent (0 results for a 47-day-old NASA conversation without it, 8 with it), same shape (root included, newest first, next_token even on a short page), 300 requests/15 min with no 1/s pacing seen. xc thread now looks up the root's created_at and pages search/all from it (wire thread_search record replaces the positional tuple); the stub serves search/all with the 30-day default and search/recent with 7 days against world.now; xsim.world --base-time puts sim-lost in August (78 days), so dropping start_time empties that thread and fails the e2e. All 3 sim threads byte-exact, x-compat 0, 14 crates under the gate (thread 428)")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_8 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 8u32, "2026-10-18T18:52Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:52 design for the remaining run step (decided, not built): xc stays a pure X client with no Cells or compiler dependency; running lives on the Cells side as mix xsim.thread_run --file F: read the program a thread carries (xc thread ID > F), evaluate it with Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate and compare each test block's verdict with the same evaluation of the original file (sequential equivalence, as xsim.run already checks), run in apps/sim/build.ps1 outside -fast on thread-sample.txt; README shows the two commands. Waits for a quieter machine (a Cells compile per cell)")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_9 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 9u32, "2026-10-20T03:51Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 23:51 run step built (uncommitted in x): apps/sim mix xsim.thread_run --file F (--program P | --expected J) parses both texts as Cells documents, evaluates each with Notebook.evaluate at F's path (memo off) and passes only when the thread carries F's cells and every test's verdict and per-target status match; runs in apps/sim/build.ps1 outside -fast on oauth1.spi against thread-oauth1.expected.json (the program the e2e proves xc thread reads back byte for byte). Probe lab/X-CLIENT-THREADS/thread_run_probe.ps1: oracle exit 0 (6 cells, 4 rust tests), tampered program exit 1 naming the 2 flipped tests. README shows the xc thread ID > program.spi + mix xsim.thread_run commands")
inl note_of_x_client_threads_10 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-THREADS", 10u32, "2026-10-20T10:10Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 06:10 follow-up: xsim.thread_run passed vacuously when both evaluations errored (status=error/error after a package type error); it now also requires the original file to pass, and apps/sim/build.ps1 proves the check both ways on every full build (oauth1 thread passes; the same program with tampered assertions must fail: 'xsim / thread_run rejects a tampered thread'). The lab probe is promoted into the build and deleted")
inl note_of_x_client_post_1 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-POST", 1u32, "2026-10-16T05:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-16 01:40 live post+delete verified on both paths (OAuth 1.0a ids 2110947129606766861, OAuth 2.0 2110948193429008887, each deleted within a second, 404 after); OAuth 1.0a is preferred because its keys never expire")
